      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Abstract:In the Critique of the Power of Judgment\u2014Kant\u2019s seminal work on teleology\u2014Kant never once mentions the philosopher who is widely credited with inventing teleology, namely, Aristotle. To make matters worse, when Kant does survey and critique available views on natural teleology throughout the history of philosophy, he seems to completely overlook Aristotle\u2019s teleology. While this may lead us to surmise that Kant was either indifferent towards or ignorant of Aristotle\u2019s teleology, the author argues that it is possible to read Kant as advancing a version of Aristotle\u2019s natural teleology\u2014a version, of course, that respects the limits drawn by Kant\u2019s transcendental philosophy. Moreover, our investigation into Kant\u2019s Aristotelianism reveals a new way of explaining the organism without rendering it a convenient fiction or dogmatically asserting its reality. In the end, our exercise of situating Aristotle in Kant\u2019s account of natural teleology reveals that omission is sometimes a high form of intellectual flattery.",
